+/*
+ * KUGO (Xperia X Compact, MSM8956, Adreno 510, chip id 0x05010000): this is ios-sailfish's driver
+ * (MIT, Pixel 1 / Adreno 530) with the SoC parts redone after the live DT and mainline Linux
+ * (gcc-msm8976.c, a5xx_gpu.c), proven first by the iSony shim's GPU probe (shim/gpu.c):
+ *   GFX rail   PM8004 S5 (SID 5, ppid 0x520, FTS2.5, apps-owned SPMI channel; LK leaves it off
+ *              at 755 mV), CPR2 corners: 865 mV covers 200 and 266 MHz.
+ *   GCC        0x1800000: oxili_cx GDSC 0x5904c, oxili_gx GDSC 0x5901c (IO clamp 0x5b00c),
+ *              gfx3d RCG 0x59000 (GPLL0 = parent 1), branches gfx3d 0x59020, gmem 0x59024,
+ *              ahb 0x59028, timer 0x59040, aon 0x59044, bimc_gfx 0x59048.
+ *   GPU        0x1c00000; SMMU (ARM MMU-500, qsmmu-v500) 0x1f00000.
+ *   A510       no GPMU power islands, no zap shader: leaving secure mode is RBBM_SECVID_TRUST_CNTL = 0
+ *              (mainline's no-zap path); 256 KB GMEM, highest bank bit 14, mainline's a510
+ *              CP thresholds and CP_ME_INIT workaround word.
+ * Everything below about the Pixel stays as ios-sailfish wrote it.
+ *
+ * Adreno 530 power-up probe for the Pixel 1 (MSM8996), boot-arg sweetgpu=1.
+ * Step 1 of GPU acceleration: get the GPU powered and clocked and read its
+ * registers. Sequence after mainline Linux (mmcc-msm8996.c, gdsc.c,
+ * msm8996.dtsi gpu@b00000; sources in research/adreno/):
+ *   GFX rail   PMI8994 S2 (SID 3, ppid 0x317; S3 is its second phase),
+ *              apps-owned qpnp-regulator. Only touched if it is off: set to
+ *              745 mV (CPR ceiling of the 315 MHz corner) and enabled.
+ *   MMSS CC    0x8c0000: GPU_GDSC 0x4034 (CX), gpu_ahb_clk 0x403c, GPU_GX_GDSC
+ *              0x4024 (IO clamp 0x4300), gfx3d RCG 0x4000 = GPLL0/3 = 200 MHz,
+ *              gpu_gx_gfx3d_clk 0x4028, rbbmtimer RCG 0x4090 (XO) +
+ *              branch 0x40b0
+ *   GCC        0x300000: gcc_mmss_bimc_gfx_clk 0x9010, gcc_bimc_gfx_clk 0x46018
+ *   GPU        0xb00000 (dword offsets from a5xx.xml): VBIF_VERSION 0x3000,
+ *              RBBM_STATUS 0x4f5, RBBM_ALWAYSON_COUNTER_LO 0x4d2
+ * Runs 40 s after start with 11 s pauses between stages, so the UFS log
+ * ring (flushed every 10 s) holds the last good step if a stage hangs.
+ *
+ * sweetgpu=2 adds step 2, the command processor (mainline a5xx_hw_init /
+ * a5xx_me_init): GPU SMMU 0xb40000 identity-mapped (smmu_identity), CP
+ * microcode from the payload (mkpayload --blob GPU-PM4 / GPU-PFP = the
+ * vendor a530_pm4.fw / a530_pfp.fw without their 4-byte header; not in git),
+ * a 16 KB ring, CP_ME_INIT + CP_MEM_WRITE of 0xc0ffee01 to a scratch page
+ * (works, 0124a). The GPU stays in secure mode (TRUST_CNTL 1): writing
+ * RBBM_SECVID_TRUST_CNTL = 0 (mainline's no-zap-shader path) reset the
+ * phone; leaving it needs the zap shader loaded through TZ.
+ *
+ * sweetgpu=3 adds step 3, the 2D blitter (Mesa fd5_blitter.c emit_setup /
+ * emit_blit): after the CP test, three blits between kernel pages, checked
+ * by the CPU: RGBA8 64x64 copy, RGBA8 -> RGB888 (the panel format, alpha
+ * dropped) and a BLIT_OP_SCALE 32x32 -> 46x46 (1.44x, the AppleCLCD
+ * present() scale) into RGB888.
+ * The GPU starts in secure mode, where the RB writes nothing to normal
+ * memory (0128a: blits complete, destinations stay zero) and
+ * CP_SET_SECURE_MODE 0 hangs the CP without a zap shader (0129a). So, as
+ * mainline adreno_zap_shader_load: TZ PAS id 13 loads the vendor a530_zap
+ * (mkpayload --blob GPU-ZAP-MDT = a530_zap.mdt, GPU-ZAP-SEG = a530_zap.b02;
+ * not in git) into the gpu_mem carveout 0x90f00000 (1 MB, outside iOS
+ * DRAM), then the ring leaves secure mode.
+ *
+ * sweetgpu=4 adds step 4.1, the first shader: a compute kernel (ir3 assembly
+ * in tools/gpu-shaders/, assembled by tools/ir3asm into sweet_gpu_shaders.h)
+ * dispatched like Mesa's computerator a5xx backend (a5xx_emit_grid): 16
+ * workgroups of 64 write out[i] = i ^ 0x5a5a0000 into an SSBO, checked by
+ * the CPU. Then blend.asm: premultiplied BGRA8 source-over (CA's
+ * vAlpha_Blend_Premultiplied_BGRA_8888) on 1024 pixels, compared with the
+ * same arithmetic on the CPU.
+ *
+ * sweetgpu=5 adds a full-frame benchmark (1024x1024 BGRA = 4 MB per buffer):
+ * CPU cache clean+invalidate of the buffers, GPU blend submit-to-fence, and
+ * the same blend on one CPU core, to size the CPU<->GPU handoff cost.
+ * 0134a: cache maintenance 0.29 ms for 8 MB, GPU 8.3 ms, CPU 4.6 ms. Now
+ * per configuration: blend.asm at 200 MHz, blend4.asm (4 pixels and 128-bit
+ * accesses per invocation) at 200 MHz and at 300 MHz (GPLL0 / 2, inside the
+ * 745 mV corner).
+ * 0135a: blend4 @300 MHz 1.7-1.9 ms = ~2.5x one CPU core.
+ *
+ * sweetgpu=6 adds the textured-quad kernel (sample.asm: affine transform,
+ * bilinear sample through the texture unit, opacity, source-over), checked
+ * against integer CPU references: an identity placement and a 2x upscale at
+ * opacity 0.5.
+ *
+ * GPU address space (for buffers that are not physically contiguous, like
+ * IOSurfaces and user memory): context bank 0's L1 entries 8-15 point to L2
+ * tables, so GPU VA 8-16 GB is mapped with 4 KB pages, handed out in 2 MB
+ * chunks (sweet_gpu_vm_map / sweet_gpu_vm_unmap; the identity map below
+ * 8 GB stays for the kernel's own buffers). sweetgpu=7 brings the GPU up
+ * (CP, zap shader, non-secure) and runs the textured-quad checks on
+ * non-contiguous kernel memory through GPU VAs.
+ *
+ * sweetgpu=8 is service mode: bring-up, then the kernel API in sweet_gpu.h
+ * (sweet_gpu_quad: one bounds-checked, cache-maintained, synchronous
+ * textured quad between GPU-VA-mapped images) for the SweetGPU user client,
+ * checked once through that API.
+ */
